  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose a Xero Alternative",
    "body": "Start with the billing unit that controls software costs as your company adds transactions and workers. Xero charges by plan tier with no per-user license fees, but it restricts document counts on its entry tier. Early caps billing at sending quotes and 20 invoices, and entering 5 bills per month. QuickBooks Online prices by plan tier and strictly caps user logins: one user on Simple Start, three on Essentials, five on Plus and 25 on Advanced. FreshBooks prices by the count of active billable clients, charging $11 a month for each additional team member. Zoho Books limits both user seats and annual invoices on every plan. Wave charges $0 for Starter software and earns revenue on card payments, while FreeAgent charges a flat subscription for US sole proprietors without publishing user caps.\n\nNext, check document and invoice limits against your normal monthly volume. If your business creates 30 invoices a month, Xero Early will not fit because invoice limits on Early count approving and sending up to 20 invoices. That monthly volume pushes you to Xero Growing at $59 a month. On Zoho Books, Standard allows up to 5,000 invoices a year, which equals over 400 invoices a month. Wave permits unlimited invoices on its $0 Starter tier. FreshBooks Lite limits you to 5 active billable clients.\n\nThen evaluate user seats and multi-currency handling. Xero includes multiple users on its plans with no per-user fees, and reserves multiple currencies for Established at $97 a month. QuickBooks Online Essentials includes 3 users for $85 a month, while Zoho Books Standard includes 3 users for $15 a month on annual billing. FreeAgent includes multi-currency invoicing directly in its single $27 monthly plan without a tier upgrade.\n\nFinally, separate core software subscription fees from payment processing and payroll add-ons. Xero offers Xero Payroll powered by Gusto for $36 a month plus $6 per employee or contractor. FreshBooks lists a payroll add-on at $40 a month plus $6 per worker. Wave lists payroll add-ons from $25 a month or from $40 a month. For more details on standalone payroll structures, see our [Gusto alternatives](/alternatives/gusto/) guide. For companies managing deposits alongside accounting, our [roundup of best business bank accounts](/roundup/best-business-bank-accounts/) covers bank structures.\n\nRun through these five checks before picking a tool:\n\n- How many customer invoices and vendor bills do you approve and send each month?\n- How many people need separate logins to view records or enter expenses?\n- How many active clients do you bill over a full year?\n- Do you invoice clients in foreign currencies or require sales tax reporting?\n- What do optional payroll add-ons, receipt scanning tools and card payment processing fees add to the base subscription?"
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "heading": "QuickBooks Online Pricing, Plans and Fit as a Xero Alternative",
      "body": "QuickBooks Online provides small business accounting across five service tiers. The Free plan costs $0 and covers 1 user. Simple Start costs $38 a month for 1 user. Essentials costs $85 a month for 3 users. Plus costs $140 a month for 5 users. Advanced costs $340 a month for up to 25 users. QuickBooks Online lists a promotional offer of 70% off for the first 3 months through November 29, which lowers Simple Start to $11.40, Essentials to $25.50, Plus to $42, and Advanced to $102 before regular monthly rates apply.\n\nQuickBooks Online fits businesses that want tiered user seats up to 25 logins. \n\nVersus Xero, QuickBooks Online enforces strict user caps on every plan tier. Xero lists no per-user license fees on its plans. To get 3 user seats on QuickBooks Online, you must choose Essentials at $85 a month, whereas Xero Growing lists unlimited invoices and quotes for $59 a month. Xero Growing costs $26 a month less than QuickBooks Online Essentials ($85 minus $59). At the entry tier, Xero Early costs $27 a month compared to $38 a month for QuickBooks Online Simple Start, a difference of $11 a month.\n\nWorked cost example: Over 12 months at regular list prices, QuickBooks Online Simple Start costs $38 multiplied by 12, which equals $456 a year. Essentials costs $85 multiplied by 12, which equals $1,020 a year. Plus costs $140 multiplied by 12, which equals $1,680 a year. By comparison, Xero Early costs $27 multiplied by 12, or $324 a year ($132 less than Simple Start), and Xero Growing costs $59 multiplied by 12, or $708 a year ($312 less than Essentials)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "FreshBooks Pricing, Plans and Fit as a Xero Alternative",
      "body": "FreshBooks centers on invoicing and expense tracking with plans priced by billable client counts. Lite costs $23 a month for up to 5 billable clients. Plus costs $43 a month for up to 50 clients. Premium costs $70 a month for unlimited clients. Select is a custom quote-based tier with no public price. FreshBooks advertises a promotional discount of 90% off for the first 4 months, reducing Lite to $2.30, Plus to $4.30, and Premium to $7.00 per month. Additional team members cost $11 per user per month. A payroll add-on is available for $40 a month plus $6 per worker.\n\nFreshBooks fits businesses whose monthly bookkeeping revolves around expense tracking and billing a predictable roster of clients.\n\nVersus Xero, FreshBooks scales its subscription price based on client count rather than transaction types. On FreshBooks Lite, having 6 clients pushes you to Plus at $43 a month, whereas Xero Early allows up to 20 invoices across any number of clients for $27 a month. Furthermore, adding collaborators on FreshBooks adds an $11 monthly fee per team member, while Xero states that it has no per-user license fees. FreshBooks payroll also has a higher base price of $40 a month compared to Xero Payroll powered by Gusto at $36 a month, both adding $6 per worker.\n\nWorked cost example: A solo consultant billing 4 clients pays $23 a month on FreshBooks Lite, totaling $276 over 12 months. That is $48 less than Xero Early at $324 a year ($27 multiplied by 12). However, if that business bills 20 clients and adds 1 employee, FreshBooks Plus ($43) plus 1 team member seat ($11) costs $54 a month, or $648 a year. Adding payroll for that 1 worker on FreshBooks adds $46 a month ($40 base plus $6), totaling $1,200 a year at regular prices. On Xero Growing ($59) with Xero Payroll ($42 for 1 worker), the same setup costs $101 a month, or $1,212 a year."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Zoho Books Pricing, Plans and Fit as a Xero Alternative",
      "body": "Zoho Books provides an accounting suite with six published tiers priced per organization. The Free plan costs $0 for 1 user plus 1 accountant, limited to businesses with annual revenue under $50K and up to 1,000 invoices a year. Standard costs $20 a month ($15 a month billed annually) for 3 users and 5,000 invoices a year. Professional costs $50 a month ($40 a month billed annually) for 5 users and 10,000 invoices a year. Premium costs $70 a month ($60 a month billed annually) for 10 users and 25,000 invoices. Elite costs $150 a month ($120 a month billed annually) for 10 users and 100,000 invoices. Ultimate costs $275 a month ($240 a month billed annually) for 15 users and 100,000 invoices. Zoho Books offers a 14-day free trial.\n\nZoho Books fits small businesses that want defined pricing tiers with known user seats and annual invoice limits, or businesses eligible for its $0 plan for revenue under $50K.\n\nVersus Xero, Zoho Books caps the total number of annual invoices on every paid tier, while Xero Growing and Established include unlimited invoices and quotes. On the other hand, Zoho Books Standard includes 3 users for $15 a month billed annually, whereas Xero Early costs $27 a month and restricts document creation to 20 invoices and 5 bills.\n\nWorked cost example: A small business on Zoho Books Standard billed annually pays $15 multiplied by 12, which equals $180 a year. Paying monthly costs $20 multiplied by 12, which equals $240 a year. Choosing annual billing saves $60 a year. Compared to Xero Early at $324 a year ($27 multiplied by 12), Zoho Books Standard on annual billing saves $144 a year while providing 3 user seats instead of Xero Early's single-tier limits. If invoice volume exceeds 5,000 a year, Zoho Books Professional billed annually costs $40 multiplied by 12, or $480 a year, which is $228 less than Xero Growing at $708 a year ($59 multiplied by 12)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Wave Pricing, Plans and Fit as a Xero Alternative",
      "body": "Wave offers bookkeeping and invoicing tools under two plan structures. Wave Starter costs $0 per month and includes unlimited estimates, invoices, bills and bookkeeping records. Starter transaction fees for online card payments are 2.9% + $0.60 per transaction, and American Express (Amex) payments cost 3.4% + $0.60 per transaction. A receipts add-on for Starter costs $11 a month or $96 a year. Wave Pro costs $19 a month billed monthly, or $190 a year billed annually (about $15.83 a month). Wave Pro is priced per business and includes unlimited receipts. On Pro, card processing costs 2.9% + $0 on the first 10 transactions each month, then reverts to 2.9% + $0.60. Payroll is an add-on priced from $25 a month or from $40 a month, though Wave's pricing page does not specify which rate applies to which plan.\n\nWave fits businesses that want unlimited free invoicing and bookkeeping records without an ongoing software subscription.\n\nVersus Xero, Wave Starter has no monthly subscription fee and no cap on invoices or bills, while Xero Early costs $27 a month and caps invoices at 20 and bills at 5. Wave charges a $0.60 fixed fee per transaction on Starter card payments.\n\nWorked cost example: Over a full year, Wave Starter software costs $0. Wave Pro costs $190 a year billed annually, compared to 12 monthly payments of $19 totaling $228 a year, creating an annual difference of $38. By comparison, Xero Early costs $324 a year ($27 multiplied by 12), and Xero Growing costs $708 a year ($59 multiplied by 12). Wave Pro saves $134 a year compared to Xero Early and saves $518 a year compared to Xero Growing."
    },
    {
      "heading": "FreeAgent Pricing, Plans and Fit as a Xero Alternative",
      "body": "FreeAgent sells an accounting plan tailored to United States sole proprietors. The regular price is $27 a month, or $270 a year on annual billing. FreeAgent advertises a promotional discount of 50% off for the first 6 months on monthly billing ($13.50 a month), or 50% off the first year on annual billing ($135, which equals $11.25 a month). FreeAgent includes multi-currency invoicing, sales tax reporting and a 30-day free trial. A Smart Capture Unlimited receipt add-on costs $6 a month. FreeAgent's public pricing page does not publish limits on user seats, invoices or bank transactions.\n\nFreeAgent fits US sole proprietors who need sales tax reporting and multi-currency invoicing included in one flat subscription without tier upgrades.\n\nVersus Xero, FreeAgent includes multi-currency invoicing in its single $27 monthly subscription ($270 a year). Xero reserves multiple currencies for its Established plan at $97 a month ($1,164 a year). A sole proprietor invoicing international clients pays $27 a month on FreeAgent versus $97 a month on Xero, saving $70 a month, or $840 a year at regular monthly rates. However, Xero lists project time and cost tracking and expense claims on Established, whereas FreeAgent's pricing page focuses on sole proprietor accounting.\n\nWorked cost example: At regular list prices, FreeAgent costs $270 a year on annual billing, compared to 12 monthly payments of $27 totaling $324 a year. Annual billing saves $54 a year. That $270 annual cost is $54 less than Xero Early at $324 a year ($27 multiplied by 12). Adding the Smart Capture Unlimited add-on at $6 a month adds $72 a year, bringing FreeAgent to $342 a year on annual plan billing. For an international invoicing workflow, FreeAgent at $270 a year costs $894 less than Xero Established at $1,164 a year."
    }
  ],
  "stillBetterFit": {
    "heading": "When Xero Is Still the Better Fit",
    "body": "Xero remains the closer fit for small businesses that want bank reconciliation and no per-user software license fees. Xero states that it serves 5.1 million customers and provides free expert setup help to onboard new businesses. Changing to a cheaper plan is permitted 1 month after upgrading, giving organizations billing flexibility as financial activity shifts.\n\nAt regular list prices, Xero Early costs $27 a month for businesses sending up to 20 invoices and entering up to 5 bills. For larger operations, Xero Growing costs $59 a month and removes document caps, providing unlimited invoices, quotes and bills. That $59 price point sits below QuickBooks Online Essentials at $85 a month for multi-user setups, and Xero includes bank reconciliation on all plans. Businesses with international transactions or project tracking can select Xero Established at $97 a month, which includes multiple currencies, project time and cost tracking, and employee expense claims.\n\nXero also offers optional add-ons to expand operations. An auto-reconcile add-on costs $5.00 a month, and Xero Payroll powered by Gusto costs $36 a month plus $6 per employee or contractor. New customers can also evaluate promotional pricing: Xero advertises 90% off the first 6 months with a promotional code through November 30. That promotion lowers Early to $2.70 a month (totaling $16.20 over 6 months), Growing to $5.90 a month (totaling $35.40 over 6 months), and Established to $9.70 a month (totaling $58.20 over 6 months) before regular prices auto-renew.\n\nWho This Is Not For:\n\n- Businesses sending more than 20 invoices a month that want to stay on an entry tier: Xero Early caps document counts at 20 approved and sent invoices and 5 bills. Those businesses should consider Wave Starter for $0 or Zoho Books Standard.\n- Solo owners who want a completely free software tier: Xero offers no ongoing $0 plan. Wave Starter and Zoho Books Free (for revenue under $50K) publish $0 tiers.\n- Businesses that only invoice a handful of clients: FreshBooks Lite at $23 a month is $4 a month cheaper than Xero Early.\n- Companies that need up to 25 user seats: QuickBooks Online covers up to 25 seats on Advanced.\n\nWhat Would Change Our Answer:\n\n- Xero removing the 20-invoice and 5-bill monthly cap from its $27 Early plan, which would make Early competitive with unlimited entry plans.\n- Xero introducing per-user license fees, which would eliminate its multi-user pricing advantage over QuickBooks Online and FreshBooks.\n- QuickBooks Online lowering list prices on Simple Start ($38) or Essentials ($85) to match Xero's $27 and $59 tiers.\n- Zoho Books altering user limits on Standard (3 users) or lowering the $50K revenue ceiling on its Free plan.\n- FreeAgent adding multi-user caps or raising its $27 monthly and $270 annual rates for sole proprietors."
  },
  "verdict": "Xero lists plans from $27 to $97 a month with no per-user license fees and bank reconciliation on every plan. QuickBooks Online prices higher from $38 to $340 a month with strict user caps, FreshBooks prices from $23 to $70 a month around client counts, Zoho Books scales from $0 to $275 a month by user and annual invoice tiers, Wave provides a $0 Starter tier, and FreeAgent delivers $27 flat-rate sole proprietor accounting. To choose a Xero alternative, count your monthly invoices, list how many users need logins, and match those totals against the published limits in the table above.",
